# git-secrets false-positive allowlist: a tracked, reviewable exemption list, read by both the
# pre-commit hook and `make lint-secrets` (see docs/ai-development.md, "Block a secret before it's
# committed"). One extended-regex line per exemption, matched against a line git-secrets already
# flagged as matching a prohibited pattern — narrow each entry to the specific fixture, never to
# the shape of the pattern itself (that would defeat the check for every other file).

# tests/test_github_app.py: a deliberately malformed PEM fixture ("nope" as the key body) that
# tests the error path for a key that fails to parse — not a real private key.
app_jwt.*nope

# .githooks/git-secrets-patterns.txt: the private-key pattern's own source regex matches itself
# (its literal text contains the string it's written to detect). A real secret never embeds this
# regex's character-class syntax immediately before "PRIVATE KEY", so this stays narrow to the
# pattern's own definition line.
A-Z \]\*PRIVATE KEY

# deploy/self-host/.env.example: the BAJUTSU_SERVE_TOKEN / GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD lines use the
# self-documenting "change-me" placeholder, the same convention `sk-ant-...` uses in .env.example
# — not a real deploy-time secret.
=change-me$

# deploy/self-host/docker-compose.yml: `${BAJUTSU_SERVE_TOKEN:?...}` / `${GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD:-...}`
# are shell/compose variable interpolation — a reference to the environment, not a literal value.
\$\{(BAJUTSU_SERVE_TOKEN|GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD)

# bajutsu/serve/launchagent.py: `"BAJUTSU_SERVE_TOKEN": token` assigns the plist entry from the
# `token` variable already validated by its caller — not a literal secret value. `[[:space:]]*`,
# not `\s*`/`\b`: this file is matched with the system `grep -E`, and on BSD grep (the macOS
# default) neither is supported (both read as literal characters), which would silently drop this
# exemption on a Mac and flag a false positive on every local commit/scan there.
BAJUTSU_SERVE_TOKEN["']?:[[:space:]]*token
